{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>But the prophet who presumes to speak a word in My name —</b> [that is,] one who speaks in the name of other elohim — even if he directed the halakhah to prohibit what is permitted and to permit what is prohibited. <b>That prophet shall die</b> — by strangulation. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 18:20), from the Sifrei (Sifrei on Judges 177–178). And the matter is not to say that if a person came and said, 'In my dream I saw, and behold Peor is telling me that you must be careful with the commandment of the lulav,' that he would be liable to death, for this one is exempt. And if he says that you should do so in order to worship that abomination — if so, this is a false prophet who prophesies in order to practice idol worship, and even if he says so in the name of HaShem, he is stoned. Rather, the meaning is that he says: 'I saw Peor, for he is the elohim, and he commands not to eat the flesh of a pig.' And likewise if he tells in his name that such-and-such is destined to happen — since he says that the worshipped entity is the one who speaks and acts, who decrees and fulfills — he is put to death by strangulation. Therefore they said in the Gemara (Sanhedrin 89a): 'One who prophesies in the name of idol worship' — such as the prophets of the Baal, who would say that the Baal is the elohim, as it is written (I Kings 18:21), 'If YHWH is the Elohim, go after Him; and if the Baal, go after him.' And they would prophesy in him: 'Go up and triumph, and YHWH will give it into the hand of the king' (ibid. 22:15) — their intent being to say that the Baal is the master who possesses this glorious name, and that he is the one who will give Ramoth-gilead into the hand of Ahab."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:46.826767508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}